"""mr-tester-ab — the tester A/B experiment: shadow testers, replay, ledger.

WHAT IT MEASURES
----------------
On an eligible slice the tier-policy tester writes the gating tests as always. Extra tester runs
(the arms: a model and an effort each, plus one replicate) write tests blind from the same prompt
and the same tree. Their tests never enter the repo. This tool keeps each arm's tests under a
local ref, replays them against recorded snapshots of the builder's implementation, and records
which arm's unedited test flipped on a game-code change. A judge that is not an arm rules which
flips are real defects.

SAFETY CONTRACT
---------------
  * `enabled` in mr-tester-ab.config.json must be the JSON value true. Otherwise every command
    prints `AB-SKIP disabled` and reads and writes nothing else.
  * Every command exits 0 (`--selftest` exits 1 on failure). On a skip or an error it prints
    `AB-SKIP <reason>` and the slice proceeds as it does without the experiment.
  * No command starts a model session. `plan` prints Agent calls for the builder to issue.
  * No command reads or writes a stop flag, a lock of the loop, or a merge.
  * Replays and seeded-defect runs take the cores `runner_env` allows, and the scoring that
    `finalize` starts runs at the lowest CPU and I/O priority.
  * It addresses the directory it ships in (config, ledger, side files), so a copy in a
    temporary directory is a sandbox.

FILES
-----
  mr-tester-ab.config.json   tracked. Arms, eligibility, caps, runner commands.
  mr-tester-ab.jsonl         the ledger. Append-only, one JSON object per line, written under
                             flock. Every row has v, kind, slice, ts. Gitignored.
  tester-ab/<slice>/         manifest, the tester prompt, the slice's gates file as it stood at
                             `plan`, shadow reports, failure output. Gitignored.
  tester-ab/judge/           per judge bundle: its key (item -> candidate or claim, label), where
                             the bundle is, and the verdicts. Never beside the bundle.
  <cache_root>/<slice>/<id>/ one plain export of the tree per shadow run; not a git worktree.
  <cache_root>/judge/<id>/   one judge bundle: bundle.md, the merged tree of each slice in it and
                             the whole test file of each test item. Nothing there, nor anything
                             it names, maps an item to an arm.
  <cache_root>/scratch/      the one directory every replay runs in; <cache_root>/target is the
                             cargo target directory they share.
  tester-ab/<slice>/score.err  why the last `score` stopped, while it has not run through since.
  refs/mr-ab/<slice>/<name>  in the game repo, local: the snapshots (base, g1, g2, m, end) and
                             each shadow's tests (its label).

LEDGER ROWS (kind)
------------------
  assignment  milestone, tier, labels {label: arm}, prompt_sha256, base_ref, tester_md_sha,
              arms_planned, skipped (null or the reason)
  run         label, arm, role (primary | shadow | replicate | followup | judge), twin, session,
              agent_id, tool_use_id, model_observed, effort_observed, cli_version, started,
              duration_s, calls, bash_blocked, tokens, cost_usd (of every session that worked in
              the export), status (ok | failed | contaminated | arm-mismatch | rerun: more than
              one session worked in the export), sessions_in_export, leak, tests_ref, files,
              non_test_files, tests (the tests the arm wrote: new, or changed against the base
              tree), tests_existing (those already in the files it edited; never run or
              counted), undetected (test lines written and no test found in them), gap
              (`no-test-file`: an ok run that wrote nothing under the test paths), test_loc
  snap        name (base | g1 | g2 | m | end), sha, source (tool | derived). base: the tree the
              testers saw. g1: the first green implementation, before any lens ran (`mark`; else
              derived as the last commit before the first lens call). g2: green after the lens
              fixes (`escapes`). m: the head the pre-merge audit saw (`finalize`). end: the
              default-branch head when the analysis runs (`score --end`).
  exec        label, snap, sha, test_id, criterion_tag, result (pass | fail | no-compile |
              not-run), reason (why a test did not run), runtime_ms, run_n, ported. Only the
              tests an arm wrote have rows. A row counts while `sha` is the snapshot's current
              commit: recording a snapshot again retires the results of the earlier one. A run
              the in-session budget stopped has no rows. A file the runner could not collect
              (it reports a failure under the file and none of its tests) is `no-compile`.
  claim       claim_id, label, type (base | spec), test, where, text, text_sha
  candidate   candidate_id, type (lens-caught | escape | base | spec | open-fail | withdrawn),
              label, test_id or claim_id, snap_fail, snap_pass, game_diff, scope (in | out),
              handed_in_session. The latest row of a (label, test_id or claim_id) stands;
              `withdrawn` says the current results no longer support it.
  quality     label, no_compile_files, flaky, not_run, brittle, brittle_files, stale_files,
              test_loc, tests, ran, gap (null | no-test-detected | none-ran), criteria,
              loc_per_criterion (test_loc over the slice's criteria: its gates entries, else the
              tags its arms used between them), dup_assert_lines, runtime_ms, bloat_usd. The
              latest row of a label stands. A run with a gap, here or on its run row, is listed
              in `status` and the report and is in no table and no decision. tests
              counts the arm's own tests as the patterns find them when the slice is scored.
              not_run counts tests no runner executed at m: a file no runner fits, a test the
              build never compiled in (a Rust module no `mod` line names), an import that did
              not resolve. Such a test can find nothing; an arm none of whose tests ran has
              `gap` set and is listed in the report. brittle counts causes, not tests: the tests
              of a file that pass at m and fail at end count once per distinct failure, a file
              that no longer compiles at end counts once (brittle_files), and brittle_tests is
              how many tests those took down; a test whose criterion the spec added or changed
              since m is excused. At end an arm's edit to a file that existed before it is
              merged into the file as the default branch has it; a forced merge whose tests
              fail or do not build counts in stale_files, not in brittle.
  mutants     label, generated, caught, missed, unviable, timeouts, wall_s, status (ok | budget |
              error | no-tests). A reported measure; nothing gates on it.
  slice       status (scored | degraded | void), arms_ok (scored arms with an ok run and no
              gap), area, added_cost_usd, cumulative_added_usd. The latest row of a slice stands.
  handoff     label, test_id, handoff_id (its candidate id): a failing shadow test `escapes`
              handed to the builder
  adoption    candidate_id, commit, handed, tests, test_added (the tests the commit added or
              changed, or `none`), lines_added, over_limit: a fix commit whose `AB-Adopt:`
              trailer cites a handed test. What the experiment itself put into the suite.
  close       reason (required-slices | max-slices | cap | backstop-days | sample-done),
              scored_slices. Written once; `plan` skips every slice from then on.
  verdict     ref (a candidate id or a claim id), item, label, scale, bundle, verdict,
              duplicate_of, criterion, wrong_behaviour, severity, scope (a base claim: in | out)
  finding     finding_id, class, status (confirmed | pending | out-of-scope | rejected |
              withdrawn), verdict, criterion, wrong_behaviour, severity, members, found_by,
              missed_by, replicate_found, fix_cost_usd. The latest row of a finding stands.

FOUND, MISSED, LEGITIMATE
-------------------------
A legitimate finding is a confirmed bug or design defect in the game code: an unedited test of
an arm flipped on a game-code change (or a counted commit names a spec claim), and the judge
ruled `bug` or `design-defect` naming the criterion and the wrong behaviour. Items the judge
marks duplicates are one finding, and so are items ruled defects on the same criterion and the
same pair of snapshots: several tests of one defect score once. A base claim counts only when
the file it names is one the slice's diff touches or a touched file imports. An arm found a
finding when its own test is in that evidence. The reference set of a slice is what the scored
arms found between them; an arm's misses are the reference findings it did not find, where its
run was valid. The replicate is in neither; the noise floor is how many confirmed findings a
slice exactly one of the replicate and the arm it copies found.

COMMANDS
--------
  eligible <slice> [<repo>]   `yes <cap bump usd>` or `no <reason>`. For mr-spawn.
  plan <slice> --prompt <file> [--worktree <dir>] [--tier <t>] [--milestone <m>]
                              Snapshot the slice worktree as `base`, export it once per shadow,
                              write each shadow's prompt into its export, keep the prompt and
                              the slice's gates file, and print the Agent calls. A second call
                              before any commit prints the calls of the shadows that have not
                              started (a transcript names the export, or the export changed);
                              the others are kept and never issued again.
  collect <slice>             After the calls return: keep each shadow's new and changed files
                              as a commit under its ref, price the runs from their transcripts,
                              flag contamination, arm mismatch and leak, copy the reports, delete
                              the exports the manifest registered, print the claim digest. It
                              lists the worktree paths a contaminated shadow named, for the
                              builder to remove before committing.
  mark <slice>                Record the worktree as g1. Run before the first lens batch that
                              follows the implementation.
  escapes <slice>             Record the worktree as g2. With `escapes_in_session`, replay the
                              shadow tests there under a wall budget and write up to
                              `handoff_limit` failing ones, arm removed, to a file for the
                              red-team, and print the adoption rule: fix a real defect, add at
                              most one test for it, cite the test's id in an `AB-Adopt:`
                              trailer. Run after the lens fixes, before the PR opens.
  finalize <slice> [--repo <dir> --head <ref>] [--no-score]
                              Record m and the adoptions among the slice's commits, start
                              `score` detached at the lowest CPU and I/O priority, and close
                              the experiment when a close condition holds:
                              a `close` row and one retro request (`tester-ab close`). A later
                              call naming another head records m again and scores it. mr-audit
                              calls it, bounded and fail-open.
  close-check [--sample-done] Close the experiment when a close condition holds, outside any
                              slice of it: mr-audit calls this on the audit of every slice the
                              experiment did not plan, while the ledger has no `close` row, so
                              the loop-active-days backstop closes it after its last slice has
                              merged. `--sample-done` closes it whatever the conditions say
                              (the supervisor, once the last sample milestone has closed).
  score <slice> [--end] [--background]
                              Replay each arm's tests at every recorded snapshot (base: claimed
                              tests only; m: twice), derive candidates and the per-arm quality
                              and seeded-defect rows. `--end` first records the game repo's
                              default-branch head as `end`. Runs what is missing; under a flock.
                              It collects first when the session ended without `collect`, and
                              records the tester calls after the primary as follow-ups. A runner
                              that cannot start records nothing: the next `score` retries. It
                              also runs what an in-session replay did not finish.
  status                      What is planned, collected, scored and spent; where each close
                              condition stands; arms with no test detected or run; stale exports.
  judge-pack                  Write every item without a verdict (candidates, claims, tests
                              still failing at m) to a shuffled bundle with arm, model and
                              export paths removed, in a directory of its own with the code
                              and criteria the items need, and print the one Agent call for the
                              judge.
  judge-apply <verdicts.json> Enter the judge's verdicts and the findings they support: from the
                              file when it exists, else from the final message of the judge's
                              transcript (the judge agent has no Write tool). A `bug` or
                              `design-defect` verdict without a criterion and a wrong behaviour
                              is rejected.
  fix-cost <finding> <usd>    Record what fixing a finding cost; five of a class replace its
                              historical price.
  report [--out <file>]       The close report, a pure function of the ledger and the config:
                              per-arm cost, found and missed by class, quality, Total in three
